% Principal Component Analysis (PCA) in MATLAB
% This program performs PCA on a dataset with at least 5 variables
% The program includes data input and output, calculation of covariance matrix, 
% extraction of eigenvectors and eigenvalues, selection of principal components, 
% visualization of results using scatter plot or biplot

%% Step 1: Data Input and Output
% Import dataset (in CSV format) using readtable() function
data = readtable('sample_data.csv');

% Display dataset and summary statistics
disp(data)
summary(data)

%% Step 2: Data Preprocessing
% Treat missing values using 'movmedian' method, which replaces missing 
% values with the median value of the surrounding data points. 
data = fillmissing(data,'movmedian',3);

% Detect and treat outliers using z-score, which measures how many standard 
% deviations away a data point is from the mean. If the z-score is greater than
% 3 or less than -3, the data point is considered an outlier and removed. 
zScores = zscore(data);
isOutlier = abs(zScores)>3;
data(isOutlier) = NaN;
data = fillmissing(data,'nearest');

% Display updated dataset and summary statistics
disp(data)
summary(data)

%% Step 3: Covariance Matrix Calculation
% Calculate covariance matrix using cov() function
covMatrix = cov(data);

% Display covariance matrix
disp('Covariance Matrix:');
disp(covMatrix);

%% Step 4: Extraction of Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues
% Calculate eigenvectors and eigenvalues using pca() function, which performs
% PCA on the preprocessed dataset. It returns three outputs: 
%   1. Coeff: the principal component matrix 
%   2. Score: the scores of the principal components for each observation
%   3. Latent: the eigenvalues of the principal components
[coeff,score,latent] = pca(data);

% Display eigenvectors and eigenvalues
disp('Eigenvectors:');
disp(coeff);
disp('Eigenvalues:');
disp(latent);

% Extract first two principal components (PC1 and PC2) for visualization
PC1 = score(:,1);
PC2 = score(:,2);

%% Step 5: Visualization of Results
% Visualize results using scatter plot or biplot

% Scatter plot
figure;
scatter(PC1,PC2);
title('PCA Scatter Plot')
xlabel('Principal Component 1');
ylabel('Principal Component 2');

% Biplots
figure;
biplot(coeff(:,1:2),'Scores',score(:,1:2),'VarLabels',{'Var1','Var2','Var3','Var4','Var5'});
title('PCA Biplots') 
xlabel('Principal Component 1');
ylabel('Principal Component 2');

%% Step 6: Data Export
% Export data tables and figures as JPEG or PNG files

% Export data (in CSV format) using writetable() function
writetable(data,'sample_data_processed.csv');

% Export figures (in JPEG format) using savefig() and saveas() functions
savefig('PCA_scatter_plot.fig');
saveas(gcf,'PCA_scatter_plot.jpg');

savefig('PCA_biplots.fig');
saveas(gcf,'PCA_biplots.jpg'); 

% Display message indicating successful execution of the program
disp('Program executed successfully!');

In Step 2, missing values are treated using the 'movmedian' method, which replaces missing values with the median value of the surrounding data points. This helps preserve the overall trend of the data while filling in the gaps left by missing values. 

In Step 2, outliers are detected and treated using a z-score approach. The z-score measures how many standard deviations away a data point is from the mean. If the z-score is greater than 3 or less than -3, the data point is considered an outlier and removed. If an observation contains at least one outlier, its missing value is filled with the value of the nearest observation. 

By handling missing values and outliers appropriately, the program ensures that the PCA is performed on a clean and relevant dataset, thus improving the accuracy of the results. context:The provided code implements Principal Component Analysis (PCA) on a dataset, addressing missing values and outliers to enhance the analysis. The program begins by importing a dataset named 'sample_data.csv' using the 'readtable' function, displaying its contents and summary statistics. Subsequently, the data undergoes preprocessing steps including handling missing values using the 'movmedian' method, replacing missing entries with the median of the surrounding data points.  Outliers are identified and removed based on a z-score calculation, ensuring data integrity.  The covariance matrix is calculated using the 'cov' function, followed by the visualization of the resulting matrices using scatter plots and biplots, effectively displaying the results of the PCA process.  The program then exports the processed data and visualizations as JPEG and PNG files for further analysis and presentation. keywords: PCA, MATLAB, data, missing values, outliers, covariance, eigenvectors, eigenvalues, pca, data preprocessing, visualization, scatter plot tags: analyze_data, statistics, data analysis
